.TH CRON-LEDGER 1 "2026-11-01" "cron-ledger 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cron-ledger \- compare crontab and timer schedules with a saved log
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
cron-ledger [--crontab PATH|-]... [--crontab-user USER=PATH]... [--system-root DIR]
            [--timer-unit PATH]... [--timers-list PATH|-]...
            [--syslog PATH|-]... [--journal-json PATH|-]...
            --since <RFC3339|30d> [--until <RFC3339|30d>] [--tz Europe/Amsterdam]
            [--tolerance 5m] [--json | --csv] [-h | --help]
cron-ledger --diff OLD.json NEW.json
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
cron-ledger reads saved crontab files and systemd timer units, works out when each job should have run in a time window, and compares that with a saved syslog or journal.
It reports
.B OK ,
.B LATE ,
.B MISSED ,
or
.B NO_TRACE .
It is an after the fact check of files you pass in.
It does not watch a live system, does not run systemctl, and does not write a crontab or any path under /etc.
.PP
A single
.B -
as a path reads standard input.
At most one path in the whole command may be
.BR - .
.PP
The text report uses color only when standard output is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset.
.B --json
selects the JSON report instead
.RI ( schema_version
1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -h\fR, \fB--help
.RB "Print the usage text on stdout and exit 0. Every other option is ignored when " --help " is given."
.TP
.B --crontab \fIPATH\fR|\fB-\fR
System crontab: five schedule fields, a user, then the command.
Repeatable.
.I PATH
may be
.B -
to read stdin.
.TP
.B --crontab-user \fIUSER\fR=\fIPATH\fR
Per-user crontab for
.I USER
(the file has no user column).
.I USER
must be non-empty.
Repeatable.
.I PATH
may be
.B -
to read stdin.
.TP
.B --system-root \fIDIR\fR
Directory laid out like a system root.
The default is
.BR / .
It is scanned only when the command names no
.BR --crontab ,
.BR --crontab-user ,
.BR --timer-unit ,
or
.BR --timers-list .
The scan reads
.IR DIR/etc/crontab ,
files in
.IR DIR/etc/cron.d ,
and files under
.IR DIR/var/spool/cron .
When the scan runs, a
.I DIR
that is not a directory exits 2.
When a named job source is given,
.I DIR
is not read.
.TP
.B --timer-unit \fIPATH\fR
A systemd
.I .timer
unit file.
Repeatable.
.I PATH
may be
.B -
to read stdin.
.B OnCalendar=
schedules are expanded.
A unit with only a monotonic trigger
.RB ( OnBootSec=
or similar) is reported
.B UNSUPPORTED_TRIGGER
and does not by itself change the exit code.
A named unit that yields no job exits 2.
.TP
.B --timers-list \fIPATH\fR|\fB-\fR
A saved timer listing with LAST, NEXT, and UNIT columns.
Repeatable.
.I PATH
may be
.B -
to read stdin.
The program does not run systemctl and does not match these rows against the log.
When NEXT is earlier than
.B --until
by more than
.BR --tolerance ,
and LAST is missing or earlier than that NEXT by more than
.BR --tolerance ,
the row is
.BR MISSED .
Otherwise a present LAST is
.BR OK .
A row with neither time is skipped.
A file with no usable row exits 2.
.TP
.B --syslog \fIPATH\fR|\fB-\fR
A saved syslog or cron log (Debian/Ubuntu or RHEL/cronie text: RFC3164, RFC5424, or an rsyslog file-format timestamp).
A name ending in
.I .gz
is decompressed.
Repeatable.
.I PATH
may be
.B -
to read stdin.
.TP
.B --journal-json \fIPATH\fR|\fB-\fR
A saved journal export, one JSON object per line, as produced by
.B journalctl -o json\fR.
Repeatable.
.I PATH
may be
.B -
to read stdin.
A line that repeats a key is skipped and is not evidence.
.TP
.B --since \fIRFC3339\fR|\fI30d\fR
Start of the window.
Required.
An RFC3339 timestamp, kept as given, or a
.I systemd.time(7)
duration counted back from now (for example
.I 30d
or
.IR 2h ).
Any other value exits 2.
.TP
.B --until \fIRFC3339\fR|\fI30d\fR
End of the window, same form as
.BR --since .
The default is the current time.
The window is half-open: an expected run at
.B --until
is outside it.
A value earlier than
.B --since
exits 2.
An equal value is an empty window and still an audit.
.TP
.B --tz \fIZONE\fR
Timezone for schedule expansion and for syslog lines that carry no zone.
An IANA name, for example
.IR Europe/Amsterdam .
The default is the system timezone, or UTC when that cannot be read.
An unknown name exits 2.
A
.B CRON_TZ=
line in a crontab overrides this for that line.
.TP
.B --tolerance \fISPAN\fR
How far a log event may fall after the expected time and still count as
.BR OK .
A
.I systemd.time(7)
span.
The default is
.I 5m
(five minutes).
An event later than that is
.BR LATE .
.TP
.B --json
Write the report as one JSON document
.RI ( schema_version
1) instead of the text report.
.TP
.B --csv
Write the report as CSV (RFC 4180, one row per audited run) instead of the text report.
Cannot be combined with
.BR --json .
.TP
.B --diff \fIOLD.json\fR \fINEW.json\fR
Compare two saved
.B --json
reports and list added and removed jobs and runs whose status changed.
Takes no other option and reads no crontab or log.
Exit 0 when the reports match, 1 when they differ, 2 on an input error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every audited run is
.BR OK .
.B REBOOT_ONLY
and
.B UNSUPPORTED_TRIGGER
are informational and do not change this code.
.TP
.B 1
At least one
.B MISSED
or
.B LATE
(including a persistent timer caught up after boot).
.TP
.B 2
Usage or input/output error.
That includes an unknown argument, a missing
.BR --since ,
a
.B --since
or
.B --until
value that is neither RFC3339 nor a duration, a
.B --until
earlier than
.BR --since ,
more than one stdin source, an argument that is not valid UTF-8, a named file that cannot be read, a named source that yields no job, a
.B --system-root
that is not a directory when the scan runs (or a scan that found crontab files and could read none of them), a window that would expand past 5000000 expected runs, or a report that cannot be written.
.TP
.B 3
No
.B MISSED
or
.BR LATE ,
and at least one
.BR NO_TRACE .
.SH EXAMPLES
The files
.I examples/sample.crontab
and
.I examples/sample.log
are invented.
The crontab is one job at 04:15.
The log is one syslog line for that job.
Run this from the repository root.
The exit status is 0.
.PP
.nf
cron-ledger --system-root /nonexistent --crontab examples/sample.crontab --syslog examples/sample.log --since 2026-10-20T00:00:00Z --until 2026-10-21T00:00:00Z --tz UTC
.fi
